course: dictionary
lesson: 643
title: Backyard: the yard behind the house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A backyard is the yard behind a house. Look at the two parts of the word: back tells you where it is, and yard tells you it is an open space outside. When you meet a new word, read the words around it, because they leave clues.
explain: «Backyard chickens are shut in at night because foxes attack in the dark.» The chickens live outside, in the yard behind the house, and they need to be locked in to keep safe from foxes. So a backyard is a place where animals can be kept outside.
explain: «On a backyard trampoline, one person jumps at a time, and never when it is wet.» Here the backyard is a place to play. The rules in the sentence tell you that safety matters there too.
explain: In the UK and Australia people often say back garden instead of backyard. If you read one of these words and the other is in the same story, they can mean the same place. Check the clues around the word, then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
fact: A backyard is the yard behind a house, often with grass.
fact: In the UK and Australia, a backyard is often called the back garden.
fact: Backyard can also be written as two words: back yard.
fact: A backyard can be a place to play, to grow food and to keep pets.
fact: A backyard can have a length, a width, an area, and sometimes a fence around it.
exercise: «Backyard chickens are shut in at night because foxes attack in the dark.» Why are the chickens shut in at night? | To keep them warm in the sun | *To keep them safe from foxes | To let them fly to the front door | To send them to school
exercise: What does backyard mean? | A park by the river | A shop at the front of a street | A house with no roof | *The yard behind a house, often with grass
exercise: In the UK and Australia, what is a backyard often called? | The playground | *The back garden | The car park | The front door
test: «On a backyard trampoline, one person jumps at a time, and never when it is wet.» What is a backyard, from this sentence? | A busy road where cars pass | *A place to play, where a trampoline can stand | A shop that sells trampolines | A kitchen inside the house
test: Which two words make up backyard? | Front and door | Tree and house | Lawn and fence | *Back and yard
exam: «Weasels crept up the carriage-drive to the front door; ferrets took the backyard through the kitchen-garden.» Which part of the house is the backyard? | Inside the kitchen | The roof | The front, next to the carriage-drive | *The back, away from the front door
